  "worked_solution": "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(3)<0\\,\u2713$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
